US-010 is implemented. All quality gates pass: `typecheck`, `lint`, `npm test` (154 tests, 12 files), and `npm run build`.

**What changed**

- **`listSales`** now returns `SalesPage` (`{sales, total, page, limit, truncated}`) and always sends explicit `page`/`limit` (default 20, clamped to the service's 100). The history page paginates on the service's `total` instead of slicing a 20-row array.
- **Staff filter before pagination.** `scanSalesByStaff()` walks the range in 100-row pages (capped at 10), filters, then slices — so the page is full and `total` is the filtered count.
- **New `getSalesSummary(filters, today)`** computes the aggregate metrics from `GET /v1/reports/sales`, which aggregates in the database, so "invoices issued" and "average ticket" no longer come from one page. `today` is the caller's local day and is only queried when it falls inside the filtered range.
- **Search delegated to the service** via `listInventory(page, limit, search)` → the `name` parameter. Sell and adjust screens now query the service (250 ms debounce) instead of downloading the catalog; the inventory table paginates against the service so its indicator and table agree.
- Also fixed while in there: `listSales`' staff-name lookup read `users` without an `organization_id` filter, against the invariant US-004 established.

**One AC I could not meet as written.** AC2 asks that the staff filter "be sent to the service". `GET /v1/sales` accepts only `startDate`, `endDate`, `page`, `limit` — its zod schema strips unknown keys, so a `staffId` param would be silently ignored rather than rejected, leaving the bug invisible. Since the story says not to change the service, I implemented the half that fixes the actual defect (filter before paginating) and surfaced the bounded-scan ceiling through `truncated` plus a UI notice rather than presenting a partial count as complete. Adding `staffId` to the service's schema would be ~3 lines and would remove the scan entirely — worth filing if you want it.

The old test asserting post-pagination filtering was replaced with one asserting the opposite, and every new test was verified in reverse: reintroducing the old filtering fails three `listSales` cases, and restoring in-memory search fails two sell-page cases. No visual check — neither the inventory service (3001) nor the app (3000) was running in this session.

